What was looked at
------------------

memmove and memcmp come from libterae's own C, in src/port/generic, which
port_memmove.c and port_memcmp.c include. The candidates found:

memmove  NuttX's libs/libc/machine/xtensa/arch_memmove.S is labelled
         Apache-2.0, but 188 of its lines appear in Linux's
         arch/xtensa/lib/memcopy.S, which is GPL-2.0, and not in
         newlib's MIT memcpy.S, so it follows the GPL copy. Linux's and
         uClibc's are under the GPL and the LGPL. Tensilica's HAL
         xthal_memcpy copies whole words and doesn't handle overlap.

memcmp   No memcmp in assembly for Xtensa exists in newlib, picolibc,
         Espressif's newlib, NuttX or Linux.

The ESP32 ROMs also hold all four functions, at addresses that ESP-IDF's
linker scripts give. A build for one of them can link with those instead
of this folder.


What is checked
---------------

Homebrew's clang has no Xtensa target and zig's has none that assembles, so
the check uses Espressif's toolchain, esp-16.1.0_20260609, with GCC 16.1
and a compiler for each chip. It builds src/ with this folder through the
Makefile, as make OS=none PORT=auto, at -O0, -O1, -O2 and -Os: for the
ESP32, the ESP32-S2 and the ESP32-S3 in the windowed ABI, for the ESP32 in
call0, and for the ESP32 with XTENSA_ESP32_PSRAM_CACHE_FIX defined. GNU ld
then joins the whole of both archives and links them with libgcc, and
nothing may be left but the compiler's helpers. Espressif's toolchain has
no libgcc for call0, so that build is joined but not linked.

Each chip's compiler defines what xtensa/config/core-isa.h reads.
__XCHAL_HAVE_LOOPS is 1 on the ESP32 and ESP32-S3, and 0 on the ESP32-S2,
whose objects then hold no loop instruction. __XTENSA_WINDOWED_ABI__ or
__XTENSA_CALL0_ABI__ gives the ABI: in call0, memcpy starts without the
windowed entry. __XCHAL_INST_FETCH_WIDTH is 4 on all three. With the fix
for PSRAM defined, memcpy and memset hold 15 memw barriers, and none
without it.

Nothing in this folder has run: before it is relied on, it needs a run on
an ESP32, or on Espressif's QEMU, as upstream QEMU has no ESP32.
